{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-167689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're told that $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1.$ According to the definition of continuity, this means that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ exists.\n\nIn order for ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)$ to exist, the left- and right-sided limits must both exist and be equal.\n\n* Approaching from the left, we have $x<1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x.$ We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}({e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & ={e}^{1}+{1}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =e+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Approaching from the right, we have $x>1,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x+a.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+a) \\\\  & =1+a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the left and right-sided limits must be equal, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) \\\\ 1+a & =e+2 \\\\ a & =e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167689" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167689" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167689" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 1, we found that ${lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)=e+2\\ne 0.$\n\nApplying the quotient rule for limits, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)} & =\\frac{{lim}_{x\\to 1}{x}^{3}+2}{{lim}_{x\\to 1}f(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2}{e+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{e+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167691" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167691" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167691" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{3}{e+2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Over the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ we use the expression $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nLet's calculate the average rate of change of $f(x)$ over the interval $[3,c]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(c)-f(3)}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{({c}^{2}-2c)-({3}^{2}-2\\cdot 3)}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}^{2}-2c-3}{c-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(c-3)(c+1)}{c-3} \\\\  & =c+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want this average rate of change to be equal to $6.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =6 \\\\ c+1 & =6 \\\\ c & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167692" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167692" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)≈\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=0.$ The step size, in this case, is $h=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ≈\\frac{f(0+1)-f(0)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(1)-f(0)}{1} \\\\  & ={e}^{1}+{1}^{2}+1-({e}^{0}+{0}^{2}+0) \\\\  & =e+2-1 \\\\  & =e+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167693" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167693" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167693" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $x<1,$ we use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x,$ which is differentiable.\n\nUsing the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}+2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167694" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167694" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167694" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e^x+2x+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We use the expression $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x$ for $x\\le 1.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is equal to ${f}^{′}(0).$ In part 5, we found that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={e}^{x}+2x+1,\\,x<1.\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=0$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(0) & ={e}^{0}+2(0)+1 \\\\  & =1+0+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167695" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167695" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "In part 6, we found that the slope of the tangent at our point is\n\n$$\nm=2.\n$$\n\nTo find the equation of the tangent, we use the point-slope form with the coordinates of $A$ and $m.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(1) & =(2)(x-0) \\\\ y-1 & =2x \\\\ y & =2x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167696" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167696" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167697" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of $B,$ the point where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $A$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The $x$-coordinate of $B$ is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167697" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The equation of the tangent line is\n\n$$\ny=2x+1.\n$$\n\nTo find where the tangent line intersects the $x$-axis, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2x+1 \\\\ 2x & =-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{-1}{2} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167697" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167697" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167697" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{1}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous at $x=1.$ Find the value of $a.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2}{f(x)}=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{3}{e+2}$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider the part of $f(x)$ that's defined on the interval $x\\in [3,c],$ where $c>3.$ Calculate the value of $c$ such that the average rate of change over $[3,c]$ is equal to $6.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $5$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/f579eb601605aa64fbc2c5bd1400a6c90ed974f15bfac81882b076ac93c443e1.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\le 1$ and the tangent line to the graph at the point $A(0,1)$ are shown above. \nEstimate the slope of the tangent using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The slope of the tangent is approximately equal to\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e+1$\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nFor $x<1,$ ${f}^{′}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $e^x+2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nThe slope of the tangent to the curve at $A$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nThe tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $A$ is $y=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2x+1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 8:\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of $B,$ the point where the tangent line to $y=f(x)$ at $A$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The $x$-coordinate of $B$ is\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{1}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/b52bd85700ddc19132ab0cbd0c9243db92bb16145c16bf9bd5b0b0fa56434d9f.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The graph of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\ge 3$ is shown above. Estimate the area $A$ under the curve over the interval $[4,7]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ rectangles of equal width.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x\\ge 3,$ we have that $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x.$\n\nFirst, let's compute the step size $Δx,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our integral are $a=4$ and $b=7,$ and there are $n=3$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{7-4}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/assets/18d2d2d79746d5782d27769b644b31f36c76fc7cf9d867788490d74935439b4e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(4)+f(5)+f(6)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $8$ |\n| $5$ | $15$ |\n| $6$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(8+15+24)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =47\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167698" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9b11ddbcc61509345a830262982669f01b67efda7130b703b3c4dd41ddf560cd"} {"question" "q-167698" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167698" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "47"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x),$ defined as follows:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}+x, & x\\le 1 \\\\ x+a, & 1<x<3 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x, & x\\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nIt is known that $f$ is continuous