  \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We want to express the area $A$ between $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x$ and the $x$-axis between $x=4$ and $x=7$ as the following definite integral:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{4}^{7}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThis definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n\t\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral above, the function is $f(x)={x}^{2}-2x,$ the lower bound is $a=4,$ and the upper bound is $b=7.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{7-4}{n}=\\frac{3}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{7}({x}^{2}-2x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(4+k\\cdot \\frac{3}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[f(4+\\frac{3k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{(4+\\frac{3k}{n})}^{2}-2(4+\\frac{3k}{n})]\\cdot \\frac{3}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059980/state.json" "file_sha256" "5c2db8152ce2ef6ebab9473e3dc3b2c72f111714c65f761fc51e9ae7eb99f80c"} {"question" "q-167699" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/